{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+6x-4y & 4{y}^{3}-4x+8y\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+6x-4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4{y}^{3}-4x+8y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=6x+6\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}+6x-4y)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{y}^{3}-4x+8y)=12{y}^{2}+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x+6 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 12{y}^{2}+8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}12 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=12\\cdot 8-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=80.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163641/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163641"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-163643/selection/63079cc748dc757e7a8e5256fd2c9d955e6cf36193b2548b0f6e7b6b72fbf55c" :answer/id #uuid "c83cccde-cf73-5a05-8d0c-b262a28012e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163643/selection/8642f29c07ed588847b818ca52d7d9b13f60105c8ca35b3f5cb1a649a54ead1c" :answer/id #uuid "104e3f2a-664f-5856-a8d6-31e370a4a310" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163643/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" :answer/id #uuid "c2802d60-5247-5d5e-8a1f-c78e7d7317aa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163643/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" :answer/id #uuid "4442e0f5-b1ca-5665-ba7a-9aaf69712685" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163643/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "66c64587-d6fa-58df-8e6e-e1b02ffd7246" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-163643/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9f1273e2-fc4a-5ad6-89c3-cbf4d4013717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-163643/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer-field/choices ["q-163643/selection/63079cc748dc757e7a8e5256fd2c9d955e6cf36193b2548b0f6e7b6b72fbf55c" "q-163643/selection/8642f29c07ed588847b818ca52d7d9b13f60105c8ca35b3f5cb1a649a54ead1c" "q-163643/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" "q-163643/selection/d0ab94490dee12664d2f70471811316d5faf74c9c2c3f6dde546976ea292b76b" "q-163643/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-163643" :question/id #uuid "e48899df-ddb7-5496-b3bb-6235b45449a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-163643" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(\\pi ,\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x))=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)+y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(y-x)+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y) & 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(\\pi ,\\pi ),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pi ,\\pi )=[\\begin{aligned}-\\pi  & \\pi  \\\\ \\pi  & -\\pi \\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pi ,\\pi )=(-\\pi )\\cdot (-\\pi )-\\pi \\cdot \\pi =0.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-163643/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-163643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-81023/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" :answer/id #uuid "ed3ea629-c721-5520-9092-d2887191c1d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81023/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "bdb473b9-20da-57f8-9535-02cbb7ffd3a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81023/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" :answer/id #uuid "4a4a17d9-5f89-5d34-9bc9-7d4bc75a793c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81023/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" :answer/id #uuid "afaa6d0a-c173-5b16-a7d6-7f98440e8b31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81023/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "a12d57b7-d98e-5281-94b3-f397e642fe49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-81023/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac66da5f-5af2-5ef4-853e-729ee2370ec7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-81023/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer-field/choices ["q-81023/selection/f853eeffac47c628af4a2b7e7b0ecd024652f548b0b0007bc18dba21ad82ad7c" "q-81023/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-81023/selection/34d6a116cfea25ad11008d41d6152ee565691a4dc671039515875488f5520d96" "q-81023/selection/83c2285763389b0bc8b13e9777da06a48782d28e9c124603d3003cc4dc2e718a" "q-81023/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-81023" :question/id #uuid "45ee04cf-eb55-5e8d-9a0d-295caa28fa48" :question/math-academy-id "q-81023" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:{{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2(x-1){y}^{2} & 2(x-1)^{2}y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2(x-1){y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2(x-1)^{2}y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x-1){y}^{2})=2{y}^{2}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2(x-1)^{2}y)=4(x-1)y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x-1){y}^{2})=4(x-1)y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2(x-1)^{2}y)=2(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} & 4(x-1)y \\\\ 4(x-1)y & 2(x-1)^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=2\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-12.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81023/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81023"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12522"] :question/problem "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(\\pi,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)$ given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/08502aa81729d4f88aec094e2e2f5c7b4a57825ef3767f1eec29700ceb6e21a3.png)" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/025dd1a0b89b52e32a3e42e5bd3492261ed060f104f627051b6950c7f94d66c1.png)\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/f1a47569220f01e1dc474a13a5040e969449ee0b10cfe7df39c3aec4c6175f41.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/f0b46d26ccc6fefc404b021bde11c4d6959c00ae0ef1145a9984da602bcc54ef.png)\n\nFirst, notice that since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/964f8ac9ca9c305faef8562f66d063f318ecbe402df337377bd9efdf61301567.png) \n we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = e^y\\cos x ,  \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =e^y\\sin x .\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)={e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{y}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & {e}^{y}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $(\\pi,0).$ This gives\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/540c920abd2989b29aae275eedd179c5118e82354915e591c0d1c5296ca9fa21.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12524"] :question/problem "Find $\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)$ for the function $f(x,y)  = x^2 e^y.$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/7444a8128c085d7bf4f56a57a60c260864a50da0b744b6dbe22a2ced89a0588d.png)\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/22dd960829ea268e5aade685b56949f8998f87ae8c040a90f04436cbfc6b9a58.png)\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/4fede2bb5c2306f62b55b7996488baee52c69e7d41b011e0e9d2b7405e63a8f4.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{y})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{y} & {x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y})=2\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}{e}^{y})=2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y})=2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}{e}^{y})={x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y} & 2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y} \\\\ 2x\\text{ }\\,{e}^{y} & {x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate this matrix at $\\left(1,0\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-12523"] :question/problem "Given that \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/b3e96b1a54af842b69da4ae5fcb44858bffdac48b27cde6677c15c05906e890d.png) \n evaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(0,1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f: \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/af5524049f5f8b3c4c4241520f2d874b454d61b1b025362f5193437f766e610b.png)\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/8882516e2732e419efdfb8439265212dd4f380d6a6ea58f6ff1291030bf50bfb.png)\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(\\mathbf x) = \\det \\left( \\boldsymbol{f}''(\\mathbf x) \\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/4fede2bb5c2306f62b55b7996488baee52c69e7d41b011e0e9d2b7405e63a8f4.png)\n\nFirst, notice that since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070131/assets/9252d1ce4f5df828f45351d01e0709cf2f2ca5df2c9c25390c111f40ca00fb22.png) we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\ln y, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = \\dfrac xy.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y})=\\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)=\\frac{1}{y}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})=-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,1) = 0 \\cdot 0 - 1\\cdot 1 = -1.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
